+# Agent Note: Mounted Chat node render acknowledgements
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-11-07-mounted-chat-rendered-nodes.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+A conversation snapshot may replace a node object while retaining its key and the mounted reader's anchor. The source publication can precede React's commit, so a DOM key, a completed animation frame or a window publication cannot establish which payload the row currently renders. A read-state consumer needs that distinction before marking source content as seen.
+
+## Decision
+
+The optional mounted Chat control advertises `renderedNodes: 1` and exposes `renderedNode(key)`. Each Node seat registers the exact public conversation-node object in a layout effect and releases it when that payload leaves the seat. `read().renderRevision` advances on these commits and releases, and the existing control subscription notifies consumers without making ChatView subscribe to individual nodes. Consumers still own focus, document visibility, viewport intersection and source event identity.
+
+The control counts concurrent seats for a node because grouped process rows can mount several parts of the same node. A lease for an older object cannot remove its successor. Disposal releases the map, and no mounted-control consumer means no render tracking. This does not alter scroll ownership, history publication, model input or Session persistence.
+
+## Verification
+
+`rendered-node-commits.client.spec.tsx` drives the real ChatNodeSeat and ChatSnapshotBuilder through a same-key replacement: the control retains the old object until the seat commits, then reports the new object without rerendering the parent. It also covers multiple seats, superseded cleanup, disposal and an unused control. `mounted-chat.client.spec.tsx` retains the existing paging, subscription and opt-out cases with the advertised capability.
+
+The same optional control advertises `readingCheckpoint: 1` and exposes `checkpointReading()` for explicit update checkpoints. `preparePageCommit()` only flushes pending scroll work and cannot prove a stable reader was sampled. The new method settles pending work, requires an initialized, measurable, nonanimating reader, captures its current semantic position and calls the actual memory provider. Tail following saves `null`; missing anchors and save failures reject. It never reads an older saved position as a substitute for sampling. `reading-checkpoint.client.spec.ts` covers fresh capture without pending scroll events, unavailable positions, provider failures and mounted-owner disposal.
